## (A) Conjecture K — CONFIRMED on the premise, GAPS on the mechanism

**Sol is right about \(g_1,g_2,g_3\).** They are constant-linearly independent in all 72 atlas fibers and at \(p=200257\) `a00pp`. Private monomials \(x_{57}^2 uW_1^2\), \(x_{57}^2 uW_2^2\), \(x_{70}\) have coefficient 1 everywhere, so a constant combination is zero only if all three coefficients are zero. Recomputed from `g_rows`.

**The extraction agent is right about two other facts**, and those are not the same object:

| Object | Fact | Status |
|---|---|---|
| 6 NF rows | rank 5, left-kernel dim 1 | Confirmed at all three primes; kernel vectors match (K.9) exactly |
| \(\{g_1,g_2,g_3\}\) | rank 3 | Confirmed; atlas already stores `cond_dim: 3` next to `nf_rank: 5` |
| max dimension | \(13\to 11\) | Uniform on both atlases |

8.S6’s sentence “one variety-level dependency among the g’s” glues the rank-5-of-six syzygy and the \(13\to 11\) drop into a linear relation among the three residuals. That identification is false. Rank \(C=2\) explains why six rows become three independent \(g\)’s (“four becomes three”); it does not explain why three independent rows drop dimension by only two. A rank-1 conormal span of three classes has **nullity two**, not one.

